!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math
!tags: Math Logic Indexed-Sets

!id: hYWnupsMW5
!front: In indexed-set notation, what are the three main pieces of \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \)?
!back:
- \( I \) is the index set.
- \( i \) is the bound index variable, ranging over \( I \).
- \( A_i \) is the set assigned to the index \( i \).

Equivalently, an indexed family of sets is a function \( i \mapsto A_i \) whose inputs are indices and whose values are sets.
~~~

!id: Mq5uspoZD4
!front: How is an indexed family \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \) different from the set \( \{A_i : i \in I\} \)?
!back:
- \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \) remembers which index produced which set.
- \( \{A_i : i \in I\} \) remembers only which sets occur as values.

If two different indices give the same set, the indexed family can still distinguish those indices, but the set \( \{A_i : i \in I\} \) lists that set only once.
~~~

!id: vJVi8zc7wK
!front: For a family of sets \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \), what does \( x \in \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \) mean in quantifiers?
!back:
\[
x \in \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists i \in I \text{ such that } x \in A_i.
\]

Membership in an indexed union means membership in at least one set from the family.
~~~

!id: xIkztH7TlI
!front: For a family of sets \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \), what does \( x \in \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i \) mean in quantifiers?
!back:
\[
x \in \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall i \in I,\ x \in A_i.
\]

Membership in an indexed intersection means membership in every set from the family.
~~~

!id: 6YXv79vcij
!front: Compare the membership tests for \( \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \) and \( \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i \).
!back:
- \( x \in \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \) means \( \exists i \in I \) such that \( x \in A_i \).
- \( x \in \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i \) means \( \forall i \in I \), \( x \in A_i \).

So indexed union corresponds to an existential quantifier, while indexed intersection corresponds to a universal quantifier.
~~~

!id: OFiotjhBDQ
!front: How do the indexed notations \( \bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i \) and \( \bigcap_{i=1}^n A_i \) relate to ordinary finite unions and intersections?
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i = A_1 \cup A_2 \cup \cdots \cup A_n
\]
and
\[
\bigcap_{i=1}^n A_i = A_1 \cap A_2 \cap \cdots \cap A_n.
\]

The indexed notation is a compact way to write the same finite operation.
~~~

!id: LKEat5q8Tw
!front: If \( A_n = (-1/n,1/n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), with \( \mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\ldots\} \), compute \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1/n,1/n) = (-1,1).
\]

Every \( A_n \subseteq (-1,1) \), and \( A_1=(-1,1) \), so the union is exactly \( (-1,1) \).
~~~

!id: bcdWRcr2ge
!front: If \( A_n = (-1/n,1/n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} (-1/n,1/n) = \{0\}.
\]

The point \( 0 \) belongs to every interval. If \( x \ne 0 \), choose \( n > 1/|x| \); then \( 1/n < |x| \), so \( x \notin (-1/n,1/n) \).
~~~

!id: X9JIdBWqzC
!front: If \( A_n = (0,1/n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} (0,1/n) = (0,1).
\]

Every interval \( (0,1/n) \) lies inside \( (0,1) \), and \( A_1=(0,1) \).
~~~

!id: oKvnT6IGXI
!front: If \( A_n = (0,1/n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} (0,1/n) = \varnothing.
\]

The point \( 0 \) is not in any \( (0,1/n) \). If \( x>0 \), choose \( n > 1/x \); then \( 1/n < x \), so \( x \notin (0,1/n) \).
~~~

!id: sBmQKVG9iR
!front: If \( A_n = [-1/n,1/n] \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} [-1/n,1/n] = \{0\}.
\]

The point \( 0 \) belongs to every interval. If \( x \ne 0 \), choose \( n > 1/|x| \); then \( 1/n < |x| \), so \( x \notin [-1/n,1/n] \).
~~~

!id: APho7K8eTK
!front: If \( A_n = [-n,n] \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} [-n,n] = \mathbb{R}.
\]

Given any \( x \in \mathbb{R} \), choose \( n \in \mathbb{N} \) with \( n \ge |x| \). Then \( x \in [-n,n] \).
~~~

!id: Cl60Y6ta3U
!front: If \( A_n = [n,\infty) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} [n,\infty) = \varnothing.
\]

For any real \( x \), choose \( n > x \). Then \( x \notin [n,\infty) \), so no real number belongs to all the sets.
~~~

!id: HFAmSKRTAp
!front: If \( A_n = (-\infty,n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), compute \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} (-\infty,n) = (-\infty,1).
\]

To belong to every \( (-\infty,n) \), a real number must in particular belong to \( (-\infty,1) \). Conversely, every \( x<1 \) satisfies \( x<n \) for all \( n \in \mathbb{N} \).
~~~

!id: v7hp1HV3jN
!front: If \( A_\varepsilon = (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) \) for \( \varepsilon>0 \), compute \( \bigcap_{\varepsilon>0} A_\varepsilon \).
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{\varepsilon>0} (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) = \{a\}.
\]

The point \( a \) belongs to every such interval. If \( x \ne a \), choose \( \varepsilon = |x-a|/2 \); then \( x \notin (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) \).
~~~

!id: Q1BbZOGnfJ
!front: If \( A_\varepsilon = (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) \) for \( \varepsilon>0 \), compute \( \bigcup_{\varepsilon>0} A_\varepsilon \).
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{\varepsilon>0} (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) = \mathbb{R}.
\]

Given any \( x \in \mathbb{R} \), choose \( \varepsilon>|x-a| \). Then \( x \in (a-\varepsilon,a+\varepsilon) \).
~~~

!id: dx2OSnIpZg
!front: If \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \) is a family of subsets of an ambient set \( X \), express \( X \setminus \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \) using an indexed intersection.
!back:
\[
X \setminus \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i
=
\bigcap_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i).
\]

An element of \( X \) is outside the union exactly when it is outside every \( A_i \).
~~~

!id: cdpC2y4RRL
!front: If \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \) is a family of subsets of an ambient set \( X \), express \( X \setminus \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i \) using an indexed union.
!back:
\[
X \setminus \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i
=
\bigcup_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i).
\]

An element of \( X \) is outside the intersection exactly when it fails to belong to at least one \( A_i \).
~~~

!id: V3SCdMtDX4
!front: Why does the indexed De Morgan law for complements of unions switch from \( \bigcup \) to \( \bigcap \)?
!back:
Because membership in a union uses an existential quantifier:
\[
x \in \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists i \in I,\ x \in A_i.
\]

Negating this gives:
\[
\forall i \in I,\ x \notin A_i,
\]
which is the membership condition for \( \bigcap_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \).
~~~

!id: 6WR8PlOKf6
!front: Why does the indexed De Morgan law for complements of intersections switch from \( \bigcap \) to \( \bigcup \)?
!back:
Because membership in an intersection uses a universal quantifier:
\[
x \in \bigcap_{i \in I} A_i
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\forall i \in I,\ x \in A_i.
\]

Negating this gives:
\[
\exists i \in I \text{ such that } x \notin A_i,
\]
which is the membership condition for \( \bigcup_{i \in I} (X \setminus A_i) \).
~~~

!id: cKsLQDON8s
!front: If \( (A_i)_{i \in \varnothing} \) is an empty-indexed family of subsets of an ambient set \( X \), what is \( \bigcup_{i \in \varnothing} A_i \)?
!back:
\[
\bigcup_{i \in \varnothing} A_i = \varnothing.
\]

There is no index \( i \in \varnothing \), so no element can satisfy \( \exists i \in \varnothing \) such that \( x \in A_i \).
~~~

!id: yDJe5RuUKc
!front: If \( (A_i)_{i \in \varnothing} \) is an empty-indexed family of subsets of an ambient set \( X \), what is \( \bigcap_{i \in \varnothing} A_i \)?
!back:
\[
\bigcap_{i \in \varnothing} A_i = X.
\]

Relative to the ambient universe \( X \), every \( x \in X \) satisfies \( \forall i \in \varnothing,\ x \in A_i \), because a universal statement over the empty set is vacuously true.
~~~

!id: GsALqf243Q
!front: What is the difference between \( \{A_i : i \in I\} \) and \( \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \)?
!back:
- \( \{A_i : i \in I\} \) is a set whose elements are the sets \( A_i \).
- \( \bigcup_{i \in I} A_i \) is a set whose elements are the elements of the sets \( A_i \).

For example, if \( A_1=\{1,2\} \) and \( A_2=\{2,3\} \), then \( \{A_1,A_2\}=\{\{1,2\},\{2,3\}\} \), while \( A_1 \cup A_2=\{1,2,3\} \).
~~~

!id: njiHfUZw2I
!front: What is wrong with treating the index \( i \) in \( (A_i)_{i \in I} \) as though \( i \in A_i \)?
!back:
The index \( i \) labels the set \( A_i \); it need not be an element of that set.

For example, if \( A_n=(0,1/n) \) for \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), then \( n \notin A_n \) for every \( n \in \mathbb{N} \), since \( n \ge 1 \) but \( A_n \subseteq (0,1] \).
~~~

!id: QP3sFSKC5h
!front: What is wrong with the statement \( x \in A_i \) if \( i \) has neither been fixed nor quantified?
!back:
The index \( i \) is free, so the statement has no definite truth value.

It can be repaired by fixing an index, such as "for a fixed \( i \in I \), \( x \in A_i \)", or by quantifying the index, such as:
\[
\exists i \in I,\ x \in A_i
\]
or
\[
\forall i \in I,\ x \in A_i.
\]
~~~

!id: pVTNLKcfFo
!front: For an increasing sequence of sets \( A_1 \subseteq A_2 \subseteq A_3 \subseteq \cdots \), what are \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) and \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) measuring?
!back:
- \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) contains everything that appears at some finite stage.
- \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n = A_1 \), because every later set contains \( A_1 \).

For increasing families, the union captures long-term accumulation, while the intersection is already determined by the smallest set.
~~~

!id: E9Y5dvQKqs
!front: For a decreasing sequence of sets \( A_1 \supseteq A_2 \supseteq A_3 \supseteq \cdots \), what are \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) and \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) measuring?
!back:
- \( \bigcup_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n = A_1 \), because every later set is contained in \( A_1 \).
- \( \bigcap_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \) contains exactly the elements that survive every stage.

For decreasing families, the union is already determined by the largest set, while the intersection captures the limiting core.
~~~
